            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The white horse symbolizes victorious conquest. In Revelation 19, it universally represents the returning Christ. In Revelation 6:2, the white horse and its rider are heavily debated, with common views identifying the rider as the Antichrist, generic military conquest, or (as a minority view) Christ and the victorious advance of the gospel. The background imagery from Zechariah establishes horses as executing agents or spirits of God's purposes.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The white horse is defined specifically in the context of Revelation 6:2 as the mount of an executing spirit, carrying a conqueror who wins the nations using a 'covenant bow.'\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Victory, conquest, and heavenly agency; with the Revelation 6:2 rider representing either Christ/the gospel, the Antichrist, or generic militarism.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"An executing spirit serving as the mount for a conqueror who brings nations into submission via the covenant bow.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book excludes the dominant views that the Revelation 6:2 rider represents the Antichrist or generic imperial conquest, entirely adopting the established minority view of a positive, Christological/gospel conquest and synthesizing it with the Zechariah imagery of executing spirits.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The book's identifications fall within established historical options provided by the consensus, though it resolves the consensus ambiguity by committing to one specific interpretation.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"By interpreting the Revelation 6:2 rider positively as winning nations by a 'covenant bow' and identifying the horse as an 'executing spirit,' the book selects and synthesizes two existing threads in the consensus (the Christ/gospel view of the first seal and the Zechariah background of horses as heavenly spirits), thereby narrowing the broader, debated consensus.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Revelation 6:2 mentions a 'bow' and 'conquering,' but does not explicitly use the word 'covenant' or specify that the conquest is positive/spiritual. Given that the subsequent three horses in the same context bring war, famine, and death, interpreting the first horse's bow exclusively as a 'covenant bow' requires relying heavily on external typological links rather than the immediate literary sequence.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
